  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 623 AM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge/sfc high pressure builds in today and lingers \nthrough Wed. Because of this, we have a nice break in active weather \ntoday into Wednesday, with partly to mostly clear skies, light \nwinds, and highs in the upper 70s, low-mid 80s. Patchy fog is \npossible early this morning, but should improve shortly after \nsunrise. \n\nA beach hazards statement remains in effect through 8 am EDT for \nLake MI beaches along the Berrien Co, MI and La Porte Co, IN \nshoreline. Waves per latest buoy obs were around 2-4 feet with 5-7 \nsecond periods (45026 Cook Nuclear/45168 South Haven, MI; sadly \n45170 at Michigan City, IN is not reporting this morning). Allowed \nthe small craft to expire as planned at 5 am EDT this morning, and I \nsuspect we will be able to drop the BH.S at 8 am EDT as planned, \nwith waves dropping to around 1-2 feet by early afternoon. Have a \nhigh swim risk for La Porte/Southern Berrien County this morning, \ndropping to a moderate swim risk after the statement expires. \nDespite wave heights dropping, strong currents are likely to be \npresent through at least early afternoon (especially southern \nBerrien/La Porte). By the 4-5 pm update this aft/eve we should be \nlow swim risk at most if not all of the beaches. With the high waves \nand winds, we had some upwelling at the beaches-so water temps \ndropped from around 66 degrees yesterday to about 57 degrees today. \nPretty chilly for August!\n\nNext chance for showers and storms looks to be Wed night, continuing \nthrough the weekend. Our upper level ridge drifts eastward and a \nbroad trough stemming from a closed low over Hudson Bay takes hold \nof the Great Lakes region. At the surface, a cold front moves in Wed \nPM and stalls out in northern Indiana/NW OH/Lower MI/IL. By Friday, \nreturn flow stemming from high pressure system off the eastern \nseaboard will bring warm air/moisture northward, which will re-\norient the front (originally from SW to NE) to be more W-E \n(generally along I 80-90). This could serve as a focus for continued \nprecipitation chances Friday into Saturday night. Models don't \nexactly agree on the broader pattern/precipitation location in this \nlate week/weekend period, so kept chances limited to 15-30 percent \nlater Friday afternoon into Saturday/Sunday.",
